P-G6, spec 001 group 8 (W1): the compose policy's relay, for the Author's hand.

Built from plain copies: tools/gates/compose_policy.py and tools/gates/test/test_compose_policy.py as P-G3, P-G4, and
P-G5 leave them (a git archive export of 7c5f1259 with W1's eight patches applied in order); edited in $TMPDIR, then
diff -u with a/ and b/ labels after this preamble; never git apply, and nothing written into any worktree. Apply it on
W1's -p branch after P-G5, with `patch -p1 < <this file>` at the repository's root, and commit the result byte for byte
(tasks.md T171, T172).

What it holds (tasks.md T106; contracts/relay-http.md §1; plan.md §16, "Group 8, as built"):
  - relay, on the tunnel end: on the edge network, publishing exactly 10.42.1.10:8080 (its edge listener, for
    CloudFront's VPC origin) and 127.0.0.1:18505 (its internal listener), in the tunnel end's class alone, with the
    release's own image, h4reet/relay by the release's digest;
  - nothing for dev-relay, which spec 000's rules already admit: built from the tree, on internal alone, no port.

Its checks, on a fresh export with P-6, P-I3, P-G1, P-G3, P-I7, P-G4, P-G2, P-I4, P-G5, P-I5, and this patch, in that
order: the policy's own tests pass, and `compose policy: PASS` on the real tree with its five env files.
